.SH "NAME"
Validator 1.2 \- PHP common data validation routines.
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
.Vb 1
\&  include("class.Validator.php3");
.Ve
.Vb 1
\&  $check = new Validator ();
.Ve
.Vb 10
\&  $email = 'cdi@thewebmasters.net';
\&  $state = 'WA';
\&  $cc = 'US';
\&  $phone = '206-123-4567';
\&  $zip = '9999-1111';
\&  $url = 'http://buzz.builder.com/';
\&  $misc = 'Now$ is the\e.*Time of "our" (discontent)!';
\&  $ip    = '206.79.157.144';
\&  $host = 'buzz.builder.com';
\&  $file = "/home/httpd/html/index.html";
.Ve
.Vb 11
\&  if (!$check->is_email($email))  { echo "Invalid email format\en";}
\&  if (!$check->is_state($state))  { echo "Invalid state code\en";  }
\&  if (!$check->is_country($cc))   { echo "Invalid country code\en";}
\&  if (!$check->is_phone($phone))  { echo "Invalid phone format\en";}
\&  if (!$check->is_zip($zip))      { echo "Invalid zip code\en";    }
\&  if (!$check->is_url($url))      { echo "Invalid URL format\en";  }
\&  if (!$check->url_responds($url)){ echo "Url not responding\en";  }
\&  if (!$check->is_ipaddress($ip)) { echo "Invalid IP format\en";   }
\&  if (!$check->ip_resolves($ip))  { echo "No RDNS for IP\en";      }
\&  if (!$check->is_host($host))    { echo "Invalid host name\en";   }
\&  if (!$check->is_sane($file))    { echo "Tainted file\en";        }
.Ve
.Vb 5
\&  $clean = $check->strip_metas($misc);
\&  $myJunk = array('!',''','=');
\&  $clean = $check->custom_strip($myJunk,$clean);
\&  $browser = $check->browser_gen();
\&  echo "You are using a $browser generation browser";
.Ve
.Vb 1
\&  if ($check->ERROR)              { echo "$check->ERROR\en";       }
.Ve
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Validator is a class containing common data validation routines.  Using this
class your programs can validate a wide variety of data types common to
forms and the internet in general.  Included with the various data validation
routines are several \*(L"common\*(R" string and array manipulation routines.  In
short, this is a class designed to conduct grunt work on submitted data. 
This version of Validator contains 19 data tests, 9 data manipulation
methods, and one array debugging tool.
.SH "METHODS"
.Sh "\fIValidator()\fR"
The new \fIValidator()\fR method simply creates a new object.  No arguments
needed.
.Sh "\fIclear_error()\fR"
When any of the following tests fail,  Validator sets the \s-1ERROR\s0 variable
with the text description of why the test failed.  Since the \s-1ERROR\s0 is
static,  the \s-1ERROR\s0 variable always contains the last error encountered.  To
clear the error, call this method.  If you want to \fBautomatically\fR clear
errors, set the \s-1CLEAR\s0 variable to true. (False by default)  If the \s-1CLEAR\s0
variable is true, Validator will automatically clear the \s-1ERROR\s0 variable \fBat
the beginning of any new test\fR.  Example;
.PP
.Vb 3
\&  $check->CLEAR = true;
\&  $email = 'cdi @ example.com';
\&  $host = 'example.com';
.Ve
.Vb 1
\&  if(!$check->is_email($email)) { echo "$check->ERROR\en"; }
.Ve
.Vb 1
\&  // ERROR now contains "is_email: Whitespace in [cdi ]@[ example.com]"
.Ve
.Vb 1
\&  if(!$check->is_hostname($host)) { echo "$check->ERROR\en"; }
.Ve
.Vb 1
\&  // ERROR now contains "", because is_hostname succeeded.
.Ve
.Vb 3
\&  // Since CLEAR is true, and example.com is a valid hostname, ERROR has
\&  // been cleared.  If CLEAR were false, ERROR would still contain the
\&  // whitespace error from the is_email check
.Ve
.Sh "\fIhas_space\fR\|($text)"
Test.  Returns true if the submitted text has spaces or tabs in it.  False
otherwise.
.Sh "\fIchconvert\fR\|($fragment)"
Internal Manipulation. Converts an (octal) numeric value into it's visual
representation of a file permission segment. (eg, 7 == \*(L'rwx')
.Sh "\fIget_perms\fR\|($fileName)"
Returns a 4 element array based on the specified file or directories file
permissions.  The array has the following structure:
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    octal permission value, string user, string group, string other
.Ve
The keys for the array are \*(L"octal\*(R", \*(L"user\*(R", \*(L"group\*(R", and \*(L"other\*(R".
.PP
The \*(L"user\*(R","group\*(R","other\*(R" keys have values matching that returned from an
\fBls \-l\fR command, so if the file's octal value were 744, user,group, and
other would be as follows:
.PP
.Vb 3
\&    "user"  == "rwx"
\&    "group" == "r--"
\&    "other" == "r--"
.Ve
Example:
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    $perms = $check->get_perms($fileName);
.Ve
.Vb 3
\&        $octal = $perms["octal"];
\&        $user  = $perms["user"];
\&        if(ereg("r",$user)) { echo "File is readable by user"; }
.Ve
Returns null on failure to \fIstat()\fR the specified file. If any specific
segment of the file's permission is mis-understood, that segment will be set
to \*(L"unk\*(R".
.Sh "\fIis_sane\fR\|($filename)"
Test.  Returns true if the file is sane.  False otherwise.  The checks are;
is_readable, is_writeable, is_dir, is_link.  If the first two fail or the
last two succeed, returns false.
.Sh "\fIstrip_space\fR\|($text)"
Manipulation.  Strips all spaces and tabs from the submitted text and
returns the altered text.
.Sh "\fIis_allnumbers\fR\|($text)"
Test.  Returns true if the submitted text contains only numbers, and returns
true if the \fIgettype()\fR test returns \*(L"integer\*(R".  False otherwise.
.Sh "\fIstrip_numbers\fR\|($text)"
Manipulation.  Strips all numbers from the submitted text and returns the
altered content.
.Sh "\fIis_allletters\fR\|($text)"
Test.  Returns true if the text contains only A\-z characters.  False
otherwise, case insensitive.
.Sh "\fIstrip_letters\fR\|($text)"
Manipulation.  Strips A\-z from submitted text and returns the results. Case
insensitive.
.Sh "\fIhas_html\fR\|($text)"
Test.  Returns true if the text submitted contained \s-1HTML\s0 entities. False
otherwise.  Tests for <, >, \*(L", and & .
.Sh "\fIstrip_html\fR\|($text)"
Manipulation. Strips all html entities, tags and attributes from the
submitted text and returns the results (plain text).  It's not a very
efficient method but it is accurate.  Don't use this method to remove html
from a file. Use PHPs much faster and more efficient \fIfgetss()\fR function.
.Sh "\fIhas_metas\fR\|($text)"
Test.  Returns true if the submitted text contains shell meta characters.
The test revolves around the \fIquotemeta()\fR method.  See the \s-1PHP\s0 documentation
for the \fIquotemeta()\fR method.
.Sh "\fIstrip_metas\fR\|($text)"
Manipulation.  All shell meta chars are completely removed from the
submitted string.  See the \s-1PHP\s0 \fIquotemeta()\fR documentation for the chars it
removes.
.Sh "\fIcustom_strip\fR\|($array, \f(CW$text\fR)"
Manipulation.  Will strip a user defined array of characters from the
submitted text, and return the modified string.  This method uses the
\fIstr_replace()\fR function, and is meta-character safe. 
.Sh "\fIarray_echo\fR\|($MyArray, \f(CW$Name\fR = \*(M"Array"")"
Debug tool.  \fIArray_echo()\fR walks through an array and echos the key=value
pairs as an \s-1HTML\s0 table.  Multi-dimensional arrays are handled recursively. 
The \f(CW$Name\fR is used to identify the name of the parent array.  It can be set
to whatever you want.  Example;
.PP
.Vb 2
\&  $config = new Config($ConfigFile);
\&  $check->array_echo($config->param, "Param");
.Ve
Array_echo is only useful for debugging.  I can't think of any legitimate
reason to use this method in a real program.  It definitely comes in handy
when debugging large multi-dimensional arrays.
.Sh "\fIis_email\fR\|($Address = """")"
Test.  Validates an email address.  Looks for standard
[user]@[hostname].[tld] format.  This is \s-1NOT\s0 an \s-1RFC\s0 822 compliant check. 
\fIis_email()\fR goes one further than just syntax, and verifies that the hostname
portion of the email address has a valid \s-1DNS\s0 record.  If the hostname portion
of the submitted email does \s-1NOT\s0 have an \s-1DNS\s0 record, it's obviously an invalid
email address regardless of it's format.  So \*(L'cdi@example.com\*(R' would return
false, because example.com does not have a \s-1DNS\s0 record.  The \s-1DNS\s0 check
requires internet connectivity to succeed.
.Sh "\fIis_url\fR\|($Url = """")"
Test.  Checks for valid http and ftp \s-1URL\s0 format. Looks for a leading
\&'http://\*(R' or \*(L'ftp://\*(R' followed by a valid hostname, followed by any
arbitrary text (or nothing following the hostname).  This test does not
check for connectivity of the submitted \s-1URL\s0 \- it only checks the format. 
The hostname check is conducted by the \fIis_hostname()\fR method.  New in version
1.1, now properly uses the \fIparse_url()\fR function to split up the url before
validating.
.Sh "\fIurl_responds\fR\|($Url = """")"
Test.  Does an \fIis_url()\fR, and if that returns true, will then check that the
\s-1URL\s0 is responding to requests.  Obviously this routine requires a working
internet connection in order to succeed.  Returns true if the \s-1URL\s0 responded
to a request, false otherwise.
.Sh "\fIis_phone\fR\|($Phone = """")"
Test.  Checks a phone number for format.  Can handle international phone
numbers.  Checks that the submitted string contains only 0-9, (, ), \-, and + 
characters.  Checks that the number, after removing any non-numeric
characters, is no more than 13 numbers long and at least 7 digits long.
.Sh "\fIis_hostname\fR\|($hostname = """")"
Test. Checks a hostname format.  Does not check to see if the hostname is
repsponding.  Looks for [sometext].[tld] at the minimum.  Only A\-Z,0-9,\-,
and . are valid characters in this hostname check.  Looks for common typos
like double dots (example..com), leading dots (.example.com), trailing
dots (example.com.), or no dots (examplecom).  Modifications for version
1.1, now properly validates the \s-1TLD\s0 and squashes a potential bug.
.Sh "\fIis_bigfour\fR\|($tld)"
Test.  Submit a top-level domain for verification. The big four TLDs are
\s-1COM\s0, \s-1EDU\s0, \s-1NET\s0, and \s-1ORG\s0.  Returns true or false.
.Sh "\fIis_host\fR\|($hostname = """", \f(CW$type\fR = ""\s-1ANY\s0"")"
Test.  Does an \fIis_hostname()\fR check and if that returns true, tests that the
host has a \s-1DNS\s0 record.  By default it checks for \*(L"\s-1ANY\s0\*(R" \s-1DNS\s0 record.  This can
be overriden by specifying the type of record to look for. (eg \s-1MX\s0, \s-1NS\s0, A
etc).  If the \fIis_hostname()\fR and \s-1DNS\s0 checks both return true, this check
returns true.  False otherwise.
.Sh "\fIis_ipaddress\fR\|($\s-1IP\s0 = """")"
Test.  Checks for valid dotted quad \s-1IP\s0 address notation.  Does not check the
connectivity of the \s-1IP\s0, only it's format.  It does check for common typos
like leading zeros or quad notations greater than 255.
.Sh "\fIip_resolves\fR\|($\s-1IP\s0 = """")"
Test.  Does an \fIis_ipaddress()\fR and if that returns true, checks to see if an
\s-1IP\s0 has a reverse \s-1DNS\s0 entry.  \fBvalid \s-1IP\s0 addresses often do not resolve to a
hostname\fR.  Do not assume that if the \s-1IP\s0 does not have a reverse \s-1DNS\s0 entry
that the \s-1IP\s0 is not valid.  This test simply looks for that reverse \s-1DNS\s0 entry
and returns true or false appropriately.
.Sh "\fIbrowser_gen()\fR"
Test.  Grabs the \fB\s-1HTTP_USER_AGENT\s0\fR environment variable and returns the
associated \s-1GENERATION\s0 of that agent. Generations returned can be any of the
following; \fB\s-1FIRST\s0\fR, \fB\s-1SECOND\s0\fR, \fB\s-1THIRD\s0\fR, \fB\s-1FOURTH\s0\fR, \fB\s-1FIFTH\s0\fR, \fB\s-1SPIDER\s0\fR,
\fB\s-1SPAMMER\s0\fR, \fB\s-1ANONYMIZER\s0\fR or \fB\s-1UNKNOWN\s0\fR.  The number of \fB\s-1SPIDER\s0\fR and
\fB\s-1SPAMMER\s0\fR agents known to the class are limited.  Should accurately peg
just about any other legitimate user agents though.  If the
\fB\s-1HTTP_USER_AGENT\s0\fR variable is undefined or unknown, returns \fB\s-1UNKNOWN\s0\fR.
.Sh "\fIis_state\fR\|($State = """")"
Test.  Checks to see if the value submitted is a valid \s-1US\s0 postal State code. 
Checks all 50 states, Washington \s-1DC\s0, Puerto Rico and the \s-1US\s0 Virgin Islands. 
Case insensitive check.
.Sh "\fIis_zip\fR\|($zipcode = """")"
Test.  Checks to see if the value submitted is a valid \s-1US\s0 postal zip code
format.  Does not check to see if it's an actual zip code. (ie, 11111 will
return true).  The zip code check looks for 10 digit zips ( 11111-11111 ) as
well as 5 digit zips.  The zip code (with non-digit chars removed) may not
exceed 10 digits or be fewer than 5.  The only valid characters are 0-9 and
the \- character.
.Sh "\fIis_country\fR\|($countrycode = """")"
Test.  And a big test at that.  Checks that the 2 character country code
submitted is a valid country code.  There are 243 country codes in the \s-1CC\s0
database. (Up from 232 in 1.1) If it finds a valid country code, it returns
\fBthe full name of the country\fR.  Returns null otherwise.  eg
\fIis_country\fR\|("\s-1TT\s0") would return \*(L"Trinidad, Tobago\*(R".  If you find a country
code that I don't have listed here, \fBplease\fR send it to me,
cdi@thewebmasters.net.
.SH "VARIABLES"
.Sh "\s-1ERROR\s0 (string)"
This variable contains the text of the last test \s-1ERROR\s0 encountered.  If a
test fails, the text description of why the test failed can be retrieved by
grabbing this variable.
.Sh "\s-1CLEAR\s0 (boolean, default == false)"
\s-1CLEAR\s0, if set to true, automatically clears the \s-1ERROR\s0 variable at the
beginning of each new test.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
see SYNOPSIS
.SH "DOCUMENTATION"
This is it.
.SH "INSTALLATION"
Copy the class file to the location specified in your PHP3.INI file. 
Failing that, use the full path to the class file's location in your
\fIinclude()\fR directive.
.SH "BUGS"
Fixed several bugs in 1.1: see CHANGELOG for details
.PP
.Vb 4
\&    Fixed: is_email() DNS bug: Valid hosts were returning false.
\&    Fixed: is_hostname() Improperly written regex. (Not POSIX)
\&    Fixed: is_country() added 14 missing countries
\&    Fixed: browser_gen() added 24 more spiders, fixed on bad entry
.Ve
